"In-region inference, open models, and new European infrastructure for sovereign AI. Mistral is driving the strengthening of European AI sovereignty through the launch of in-region inference endpoints, plans to secure large-scale compute infrastructure in Europe, and support for third-party open models. Official launch of Mistral Regional Endpoints, allowing users to choose inference execution regions between Europe and the US, alongside the announcement of the Mistral Priority Tier offering uptime SLAs Formation of a coalition aiming to build up to 1 GW of European AI compute infrastructure by 2030 to secure long-term computing capacity Expansion of support beyond proprietary models to third-party open models, starting with Z.ai's GLM-5.2, within the same infrastructure and regional control environment Mistral x HUMAIN Mistral AI has entered into a strategic partnership worth hundreds of millions of euros with HUMAIN to strengthen sovereign AI capabilities in Saudi Arabia and the Middle East region. Promoting strategic collaboration across AI infrastructure construction, cutting-edge model development, and AI solution deployment targeting Saudi Arabia and the Middle East region Focusing on localization and the development of frontier models with superior Arabic language performance, starting with cybersecurity and voice sectors Establishing a joint go-to-market strategy to introduce sovereign AI—providing customers with control over data, computation, and operations—to regulated industries such as finance, manufacturing, telecommunications, and the public sector Agentic Search. More accurate and efficient results from your AI systems. Mistral AI has introduced 'Agentic Search,' a multi-step search layer designed to efficiently explore and verify complex enterprise documents and data. Provides a multi-step search loop that goes beyond simple chunk retrieval to explore, browse, and verify long, complex documents and multiple data sources. Operates on top of existing search indices using five tools—search, open, navigate, read, and grep—while supporting secure isolated environments. Available via the Mistral Search Toolkit, Studio, and Vibe Libraries, significantly reducing latency and token usage. Introducing Shieldstral. Mistral AI has released Shieldstral, a 3B open-weight multimodal safety classifier that flexibly evaluates text and image safety via natural language queries without retraining. It redefines content moderation as a policy-adaptive question answering (QA) task, directly taking natural language policies as input during inference to provide an integrated safety evaluation of text and images without retraining. It matches or outperforms guardrail models up to 7x larger in text safety and establishes a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) in multimodal safety moderation. Its open weights are released under the Apache 2.0 license, allowing efficient execution on a single 16GB NVIDIA GPU. Leanstral 1.5: Proof Abundance for All Mistral AI has released Leanstral 1.5, an open-source model that significantly improves performance in formal verification and Lean 4 proof engineering. Featuring 119B total parameters with 6B active parameters, it has been released on Hugging Face and via a free API under the Apache-2.0 license. Trained through continual pre-training, supervised fine-tuning, and CISPO-based reinforcement learning, it was developed in a coding agent environment capable of multi-turn proving and directly interacting with file systems. It demonstrated practical code verification capabilities by saturating the miniF2F benchmark, solving the majority of PutnamBench problems, and discovering 5 previously unknown bugs across 57 real-world open-source repositories. MG Ship adds AI route optimisation as logistics returns accelerate Logistics company MG Ship has introduced an AI-powered route optimization and carrier recommendation module, driven by supply chain cost and time savings. MG Ship has deployed an AI module on its supply chain platform for global retailers and shippers, combining route optimization algorithms with carrier recommendation capabilities. AI adoption in logistics is moving beyond the proof-of-concept stage, demonstrating tangible operational improvements and short-term ROI across dynamic route planning, demand forecasting, and document automation. The system analyzes real-time cargo data, weather conditions, port congestion, and carrier performance metrics to provide low-cost, low-risk route recommendations and scenario simulations. IFM Releases K2 Horizon: Six Apache 2.0 Models From 0.9B to 375B IFM has released K2 Horizon under the Apache 2.0 license, a fully open-source language model family consisting of six models ranging from 0.9B to 375B, complete with pre-training data, training code, and checkpoints. IFM fully open-sourced six model sizes ranging from 0.9B to 375B-A23B, along with the pre-training corpus, intermediate checkpoints, and training code under the Apache 2.0 license. Each model was pre-trained on approximately 20 trillion tokens, introducing the MoVA architecture that applies sparsity to attention, as well as the Uno LoRA adapter that delivers a 3x decoding speedup without quality degradation. Sharing the same architecture and serving environment enables seamless scaling from prototypes (3.7B) to large models (375B), with out-of-the-box support for various frameworks including vLLM, SGLang, and Ollama. From Matching Models to Recruiting Agents: A Systematized Narrative Review of AI Recruitment Systems, Evaluation, and Governance This paper outlines the evolution of AI recruitment systems from simple profile-matching and ranking models to tool-using recruiting agents that execute multi-step workflows, addressing key evaluation and governance challenges. It analyzes the trajectory of AI recruitment technology progressing from bidirectional retrieval and neural matching to large language model (LLM)-based components and recruiting agents. It synthesizes three core shifts: from simple similarity to mutual compatibility, from single models to compound workflows, and from offline prediction to evidence- and productivity-based evaluation. It highlights limitations in existing research—such as bias in behavioral labels, validity constraints stemming from proprietary and synthetic data, and the failure to holistically evaluate utility, fairness, privacy, and security—while offering future directions for governance and evaluation. Harbor Adapters and Harbor-Index: Infrastructure and a Curated Meta-Dataset for Large-Scale Agentic Evaluation This study proposes Harbor Adapters, an infrastructure for unified evaluation across diverse agent benchmarks, and Harbor-Index, a curated meta-dataset of high-difficulty tasks. Developed Harbor Adapters, a unified infrastructure capable of interfacing and evaluating more than 80 benchmarks with arbitrary agents, validated through code reviews and equivalence testing Conducted large-scale evaluations across 54 benchmarks on 8 models to deeply analyze agent capabilities and failure modes Released Harbor-Index, a cost-effective yet challenging meta-dataset consisting of 82 carefully curated, high-difficulty, high-quality tasks across 29 benchmarks Data-Optimized Contingency Screening: A Machine Learning Approach to Power System Security This study classifies security levels of contingencies using machine learning-based data optimization techniques to ensure the stability of power systems. Contingency data was extracted based on the Newton-Raphson power flow method and the Overall Performance Index (OPI), followed by preprocessing using SMOTE for class imbalance resolution and PCA for dimensionality reduction. KNN, Random Forest, and SVM algorithms were evaluated under N-k (k=1, 2, 3) scenarios on IEEE-14 and IEEE-30 bus systems. Random Forest achieved the highest F1 score, and PCA contributed more significantly to model performance improvement than SMOTE. A Removal Based Approach to Improve LLM Faithfulness at Test-Time This study proposes a test-time approach that addresses the incompleteness dimension of faithfulness by removing omitted elements from the input and re-querying the model. The unfaithfulness of LLM explanations is formulated along two dimensions: 'incompleteness,' where factors influencing the answer are omitted, and 'unsoundness,' where factors with no actual effect are mentioned. Unlike existing approaches, it removes hidden confounders without model weight modifications or fine-tuning, by deleting concepts not mentioned in the explanation from the input and re-querying the model. It demonstrates improved explanation faithfulness compared to baseline prompting across two datasets, diverse model families, and two independent faithfulness metrics. Why Better Models Can Create Riskier Systems: Evidence from LLM Agents in Financial Markets This study reveals that even as the performance of individual LLMs improves, system-level risks—such as in financial markets—can actually increase due to heightened behavioral correlation among models. Due to shared training data and architectures, higher-performing, state-of-the-art LLMs exhibit stronger behavioral correlation and act more similarly. While agent participation lowers risk in accurate reasoning environments, the same correlated behavior acts as a systemic risk factor under distorted information environments. A 'capability paradox' emerges, where improvements in individual model capabilities do not translate into overall system improvements. ProToMEx: Rapid, Interpretable Explanations via Structured Representations This research introduces ProToMEx, a model-agnostic explanation framework that leverages probabilistic topic models to structurally interpret complex classification rationales and significantly improve the speed of explanation generation. To overcome the limitations of simple feature importance-based explanations, it applies Probabilistic Topic Models (PTMs) to learn higher-level, meaningful decision rationales. It provides both global explanations that describe the overall behavior of the model and local explanations that disentangle multiple causes behind individual predictions. While maintaining fidelity on par with SHAP and LIME, it drastically reduces the computational cost required to generate local explanations, making it suitable for real-time applications. A Data Fusion Framework for Grounding Aerospace Surrogate Model via Experimental Wind-Tunnel Observations This study introduces an experimental calibration framework that significantly improves prediction accuracy by fusing a CFD simulation-based aerodynamic surrogate model with wind-tunnel experimental data. Proposed a separate calibration network that learns the error with actual wind-tunnel experiment (PSP) observations without retraining the deep learning surrogate model pretrained on high-fidelity CFD data. Significantly reduced prediction errors in wing suction peak, shock wave location, and pressure recovery regions at Mach 0.85, demonstrating high accuracy even under unlearned angle-of-attack conditions. Effectively bridges the systematic gap between simulation and real-world experiments using only a small amount of experimental data while maintaining the computational efficiency and generalization performance of the pretrained surrogate model. Quantum-Assisted Memory-Efficient Training for Parameter-Intensive Wi-Fi-Based Human Activity Recognition This study presents a Quantum-Assisted Memory-Efficient Training framework (Q-MET) to resolve memory inefficiencies occurring during the training and inference processes of Wi-Fi-based human activity recognition (HAR) models. By utilizing a hybrid quantum-classical neural network to indirectly generate parameters, it significantly reduces the number of trainable parameters compared to conventional backpropagation-based deep learning. To support deployment on resource-constrained devices, it achieves lightweight inference by integrating structured pruning techniques during the training stage. It secures substantial model sparsity while maintaining or surpassing the classification accuracy of conventional classical models. Evaluating Large Language Models for Forced Outage Risk Prediction: Benefits and Comparison to Machine Learning This study evaluates the zero-shot capabilities of large language models (LLMs) to predict weather-driven forced outage risks in distribution grids without labeled training data, comparing their performance to traditional supervised learning models. Binary severity classification was conducted across 3-hour, 6-hour, and 12-hour forecast horizons using six years of outage records and high-resolution weather data from Central Texas. While traditional supervised learning classifiers outperformed zero-shot LLMs in terms of macro-F1 and precision, recent-generation LLMs demonstrated competitive performance. Beyond accuracy, LLMs offer complementary strengths in actionable reasoning and geographic transferability, suggesting that combining them with supervised models may represent the best practical approach. Data-Driven Learning of Unknown Nonlinear Differential Equations Using Functional Analysis This study introduces a novel interpretable machine learning approach that leverages functional analysis and operator theory to learn unknown nonlinear differential equations from a single trajectory dataset without prior physical knowledge. It is formulated based on functional analysis and operator theory, employing an integral distance between functions in a function space as the cost function instead of a discrete sum of errors. It learns unknown nonlinear dynamic vector fields from just a single state trajectory dataset without prior physical knowledge, and proposes an incremental learning algorithm that processes new samples in an online manner. It is applicable to both autonomous and non-autonomous dynamical systems with or without external forces, enabling the simultaneous discovery of unknown underlying dynamics and time-varying external forces. Evidence Integration in Large Language Models This study investigates the distributional theory and mechanisms behind how Large Language Models (LLMs) integrate externally provided evidence into their existing judgments and response distributions. LLMs tend to more readily accept external evidence when it aligns with their own higher prior probabilities or inherent error patterns. Even when internal verification identifies errors or invalidity, models still integrate external candidate answers, demonstrating that evidence integration is a receiver-centric control mechanism rather than simple confidence scoring. Causal intervention analysis revealed that structural stages accepting and conveying external candidates execute in the latter layers of the network, showing that verification representations are decoupled from actual evidence integration states. MedProb: Probing Internal Representations of Vision-Language Models for Medical Question Answering This study introduces MedProb, a framework that lightly probes the frozen internal representations of vision-language models to evaluate medical question-answering performance and extract latent signals. Through the MedProb framework, which directly predicts multiple-choice answers from frozen VLM representations without relying on free-text generation, the researchers demonstrated superior performance compared to conventional prompting, specialized medical VLMs, and agentic systems. Benchmark evaluations on datasets such as PATH-VQA, SLAKE, and VQA-RAD showed that the probing technique narrows the performance gap between small and large models, revealing that substantial information remains latent within smaller models. A comparison across 14 matched general-purpose and medical VLM pairs revealed that medical domain adaptation does not consistently improve linear decodability, showing different behavior compared to the answer-position bias of up to 10 percentage points observed in traditional text generation approaches. You Really Didn't Get That? Benchmarking Social Pragmatic Inference for Indirect and Playful Chinese Online Comments This study presents a new benchmark evaluating whether large language models can properly infer the indirect and playful social pragmatic meanings of Chinese online comments, where context is difficult to grasp. Constructed 4,735 human-verified diagnostic items by reconstructing preceding contexts and plausible misinterpretation options from over 200,000 Chinese social media interaction records. Evaluation of eight large language models showed that the best-performing model achieved 81.42% accuracy and the overall average was 68.70%, lagging significantly behind human performance of 90.8%. Models demonstrated limitations by capturing general irony or playful nuances while misidentifying specific operational mechanisms or interaction intents. A Systematic Evaluation of Cross-Lingual Consistency Enhancement Methods in Multilingual Language Models This study systematically compares and evaluates techniques for improving cross-lingual response consistency in multilingual language models within a standardized environment. Post-training approaches were generally more stable than inference-time intervention methods, with direct distribution alignment techniques in particular consistently improving consistency across all model and dataset combinations. Cross-domain transfer performance was found to be limited unless the source and target tasks shared similar output formats. For culture-dependent questions, there was no performance degradation in controlled closed-ended evaluations, but intermittent drops in accuracy were observed in open-ended generation settings, especially in non-English responses. GRACE: Graph-Grounded Reflective Agent Copilot Engine for Expert-in-the-Loop Knowledge Expansion This study proposes GRACE, a framework that integrates graph structures with expert-in-the-loop collaboration to mitigate LLM hallucinations and expand knowledge bases. It decomposes LLM responses into atomic claims, connects them with a trusted knowledge dictionary via a weighted bipartite graph, and classifies the claims as Grounded, Refuted, or Boundary. It designs a Return on Attention (RoA) objective function to enable efficient resource allocation, routing claims to expert review only when priority-weighted uncertainty exceeds verification costs. Claims verified by experts are promoted to new evidence anchors, continuously expanding the knowledge base and outperforming existing RAG baselines. GrapheneOS to Overhaul Default Apps and Secure Clipboard GrapheneOS, a security-focused mobile operating system, is modernizing its default messaging app, overhauling other default apps, and pursuing standalone RCS support. The UI of the default Messaging app is being completely rewritten in Android Compose, with a long-term goal of supporting MLS-based end-to-end encrypted (E2EE) RCS that operates independently of Google Messages. There are plans to overhaul or entirely replace the remaining default apps in the near future, including the outdated AOSP Gallery and Keyboard. Resources are expanding from the previous OS-centric focus to default bundled apps, accelerating development speed through recent and additional hiring. Making Simplicity Easy (2011) It examines the distinction between 'easy'—meaning subjective familiarity or convenience in software development—and 'simple'—an objective structural state free of intertwining between elements—while emphasizing the critical importance of simple design. Simplicity refers to an objective structure where elements are not entangled, whereas easiness is a relative concept shaped by an individual's familiarity and accessibility. Testing and type checking serve merely as safety nets; they cannot substitute for human understanding and fundamental reasoning about software systems. Going beyond mere modularity or encapsulation to clearly separate roles and responsibilities and minimize entanglement is essential for securing long-term reliability and maintainability. Alien Mind: An Unfamiliar Intelligence Distinct from Humans It suggests that development speed must be constrained to match safety and alignment capabilities, and international coordination must be established, to respond to the rapid advancement of AI reasoning models and the risks of recursive self-improvement. The likelihood of AI model advancement entering the recursive self-improvement (RSI) stage has increased, but value alignment and monitoring capabilities are failing to keep pace. Because chain-of-thought monitoring alone has limitations, focus must be placed on alignment and monitoring technologies and defensive system construction, with voluntary slowdowns and development pauses considered if necessary. Broad institutional intervention is needed beyond the level of individual companies, such as mandating common safety standards and government-level international cooperation. GPT-6 Astra Achieves First Perfect Score Across All Sections in CSAT Benchmark An analysis revealing that GPT-6 Astra scored a perfect mark across all sections for the first time in history in an unofficial 2026 CSAT LLM evaluation. In standard mode, GPT-6 Astra achieved a perfect score of 450 points across all subjects, including Korean, Mathematics, English, Korean History, and four Inquiry electives. Its output tokens were relatively low at about 58% of input tokens, but in batch-input mode across subjects, it missed Question 8 in Society and Culture, scoring 448.5 points. Although evaluated without external tools, it is difficult to definitively conclude pure unlearned reasoning capability due to the possibility that CSAT questions were included prior to the knowledge cutoff. The Incumbents Are Coming: Where Can Specialized AI Startups Win? An analysis of strategies for vertical, specialized AI startups to secure competitiveness and survive against incumbent software giants deploying AI agents. Incumbents holding Systems of Record, such as Salesforce and Docusign, are expanding their own agents to strengthen data control and pricing power. Because incumbent AI easily remains tied to record-centric tasks within proprietary systems, vertical startups must seek opportunities in end-to-end task completion that crosses system and organizational boundaries. Successful specialized AI must go beyond mere data retention by incorporating domain experts' revision rationale and final outcomes into learning loops, scaling from small, iterative tasks to sophisticated workflows. KV cache as an agent runtime [R] Introduction to a research approach that utilizes the KV cache—a model's inference state—as an agent runtime to enhance the interactivity and responsiveness of LLM systems. Proposes a method to achieve higher interactivity in LLMs by directly modifying the model's inference state (KV cache). Following previous research on 'Hogwild! Inference' and 'AsyncReasoning', it previews follow-up work applying similar techniques to enable a Qwen3.8-27B agent to interactively play in a DOOM environment. Emphasizes that between abstract harnesses and costly model modifications, model inference/runtime design itself can serve as a crucial pillar for expanding agent capabilities. Roboticists working in Learning-from-Demonstrations and Behavioral Cloning : What is going on in your field these days? [D] A community discussion asking about the impact of recent advancements in frontier LLMs and VLAs on Learning from Demonstration (LfD) and Behavioral Cloning (BC) research in robotics. Asks whether recent advances in frontier LLMs directly influence LfD and BC research, or if the field is progressing in an independent direction Seeks opinions from practicing roboticists regarding practical applications and use cases of Vision Transformers (ViT) and Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models Inquires about other notable technical advancements currently gaining traction in this subfield Automotive Radar Object Classification [P] Experimental results and analysis on classifying five object classes, including vehicles and pedestrians, using a 3-layer MLP based on single-scan radar point cloud histograms from the RadarScenes dataset. Classified five object classes (car, large_vehicle, two_wheeler, pedestrian, pedestrian_group) using the RadarScenes dataset, a 16-bin per-scan histogram input, a 3-layer MLP, and class-weighted cross-entropy loss. Macro F1 score improved significantly from 0.381 to 0.764 as the number of detection points per instance increased from 1 to 5, with car and pedestrian showing the highest classification performance and two_wheeler showing the lowest. Stationary or idling two-wheelers easily misclassify as pedestrians due to overlapping velocity distributions, and biases in dataset splits had a greater impact on performance than variations in model architecture or histogram configurations. Measuring LLM performance drift: observations and methodology from 31,352 repeated benchmark measurements [D] Presents observations and a longitudinal benchmark methodology based on 31,352 repeated measurements to track performance changes and drift of API-based LLMs over time. Introduced a longitudinal measurement approach that continuously tracks performance changes driven by API model infrastructure and version updates, replacing traditional one-off leaderboard benchmarks. Confirmed that measuring performance fluctuations over time is essential, as the variation in daily medians across different dates was roughly three times larger than the score variation within the same day. Adopted a design that maintains the full evaluation dataset as private to prevent benchmark contamination, while ensuring transparency regarding the measurement design and methodology. The complex corporate web behind a $3.2 billion AI data center A fire at a $3.2 billion mega AI data center located in Somerset, New York, revealed a complex ownership structure, inadequate safety management, and issues surrounding liability. Severe safety management failures were confirmed at the Lake Mariner data center fire scene, including non-functioning alarms, a lack of fire suppression systems, and dead fire hydrants. There was a complete absence of an on-site information-sharing system, with firefighters deployed into suppression efforts without access to hazardous chemical safety documentation. The data center involves an intricately intertwined web of corporate contracts and equity structures among TeraWulf, Fluidstack, Google, Anthropic, and others. Presentation: From AI Agent Demo to Production: Automated Testing and Evaluation It addresses why AI agents remain stuck in the demo stage and introduces methodologies for deploying them to production environments through simulation-based automated testing and evaluation. Approximately 95% of AI agents remain as interesting demos and fail to deliver tangible value in actual production stages. Simulation-based testing utilizing synthetic user personas, trajectory entropy, and automated CI/CD pipelines can resolve stability and compliance issues in multi-turn agents. Drawing on experience from Columbia University laboratories and the startup Arklex AI, it presents approaches to catching edge cases before deployment and scaling self-learning workflows. Your Cloud Security Checklist Doesn't Work the Way You Think It Does Introduces analysis findings showing that the risk profiles for vulnerabilities and misconfigurations differ significantly across major cloud service providers (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud). Weak IAM controls and missing logging are universal issues affecting 80% to 98% of accounts regardless of cloud provider. AWS was the most vulnerable and Google Cloud was the safest in the categories of exposed services, permissive firewalls, and weak encryption, while Azure had the highest rate of misconfigured services at 80%. A higher variety of cloud services increases the likelihood of configuration errors, and Google Cloud exhibited a relatively lower risk prevalence thanks to its Shared Fate model featuring robust default security settings. ChatGPT Tests 'Personal Writing Style' Learning Feature... \"Moving Beyond the AI-Sounding Tone\" OpenAI is testing a ChatGPT 'Writing Style' feature that analyzes actual documents and messages written by users to craft text reflecting their unique writing style and tone. OpenAI is testing a 'Writing Style' feature that integrates external documents, emails, and messaging app data to learn a user's unique writing habits and tone. Instead of having to describe styles through prompts as before, it analyzes actual written text data to tailor and embody the 'user's voice,' reducing the distinctive AI writing feel. The feature is currently being tested on a limited basis with a small user group under the 'Writing' menu within ChatGPT's 'Personalisation' settings, while specific integration details remain undisclosed. Hikers Stranded After Following 'Gemini' AI Advice Rescued the Next Day This article covers an incident where hikers who relied too heavily on advice from Google's generative AI 'Gemini' to plan their trek became stranded in the mountains and were rescued the following day. Three 20-year-old men climbing Mount Shasta in California (elevation 14,179 feet) prepared only 8 hours' worth of food and water based on Gemini's advice, but became stranded after the hike took more than 16 hours. The group went off course during their descent due to dead phone batteries and a malfunctioning power bank, spent the night outdoors, and was rescued the next morning with the help of a rescue team. A Forest Service ranger emphasized that while AI can be a useful tool, it should not be the sole tool, highlighting that critical thinking suited to on-site conditions and verification with experts are essential. Warning from OpenAI's Chief Scientist: \"Alien-like AI, We Must Prepare for Self-Improvement\" OpenAI's Chief Scientist warned of the risks associated with the rapid advancement of AI and its entry into the recursive self-improvement phase, raising the need for safety guardrails and pacing development speed. If the pace of AI advancement continues, it is highly likely to enter the Recursive Self-Improvement (RSI) stage within years, where machines lead AI development themselves. AI is difficult to understand as it operates like an 'alien mind' distinct from human intelligence, and existing CoT monitoring and reward learning methods face clear limitations. To prevent the risk of autonomous misaligned behavior, establishing common safety standards—such as developing defensive AI, third-party audits, and Responsible Scaling Policies (RSP)—as well as voluntary pacing of development speed, is necessary. GitHub Unveils AI Coding Routing System 'HydraFusion'... \"Cutting Costs While Maintaining Quality\" GitHub has unveiled 'Project HydraFusion,' a coding agent routing system that dynamically combines multiple AI models and execution patterns per task in real time to drastically reduce costs while maintaining frontier-level quality. Goes beyond simple model routing by dynamically combining three execution patterns—single, cascade, and critique—at runtime to determine the optimal problem-solving approach Achieved a 67% cost reduction compared to using Claude Opus 5 alone on TerminalBench 2.1, while improving verified quality by 4.9 percentage points Implemented safeguards for cost control and execution stability, such as per-stage cost and time limits, as well as critique within isolated environments SpaceXAI Unveils 'Grok Imagine Video 1.5' with Enhanced Video Continuity and Quality SpaceXAI has unveiled 'Grok Imagine Video 1.5', a video generation agent that significantly improves scene-to-scene continuity and character consistency. Rather than releasing a completely new model, it adds an orchestration layer on top of the existing 'Video 1.5' to plan and connect scenes. Based on the 'Image 2.0' model, it utilizes up to 5 reference images to maintain multi-shot continuity and consistency across characters, backgrounds, and props. Currently, Video 1.5 can generate video and audio up to 15 seconds long, serving as a storytelling environment that connects multiple short scenes. AI Data Centers Reshaping Rural U.S. Land Prices: First-Half Land Acquisitions Reach 8.07 Trillion KRW Driven by the AI data center development boom across the United States, first-half land acquisition volumes have surged, escalating conflicts and regulatory scrutiny over infrastructure expansion, including rising electricity rates and resident backlash. In the first half of this year, U.S. data center land acquisitions surged 79% year-over-year to approximately $6 billion, accounting for 27% of all development land. The sharp increase in power demand from data centers has driven up capacity market prices, with an estimated $23.1 billion in additional cost burdens through 2028. Concerns over rising electric bills, water resource depletion, and farmland loss have prompted local governments, including New York State, to introduce moratoria on new hyperscale data center construction. Gmarket Also Enters 'Agentic Commerce'... How Does It Differ from Naver and Kakao? Gmarket has officially joined the AI agentic commerce market competition alongside Naver and Kakao by unveiling a ChatGPT-based shopping plugin. Gmarket has begun offering hyper-personalized product recommendations via a ChatGPT plugin and is pursuing the development of four proprietary AI agents (Chat, Review, Search, and Discovery) in the future. Naver is strengthening its ecosystem by linking its proprietary shopping AI agent with membership perks (early-morning delivery and unlimited free shipping), while Kakao is preparing an end-to-end ordering and payment experience within KakaoTalk. Although all three platforms provide conversational product recommendations and aim for eventual payment integration, none have fully implemented checkout functionality at this stage. Jensen Huang: \"GPT-6 Astra Ushers in the AGI Era… Powered by 100,000 GPUs\" Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang evaluated OpenAI's latest AI model 'GPT-6 Astra' as AGI, revealing that more than 100,000 of Nvidia's GPUs were utilized to train the model. Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang characterized OpenAI's new model 'GPT-6 Astra' as AGI (Artificial General Intelligence), stating that the era of AGI has finally begun. More than 100,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell NVL72 units were deployed to train GPT-6 Astra, with an additional 400,000 units scheduled to be brought online. Industry analysts interpret Huang's remarks as an effort to highlight the pivotal role of Nvidia's GPU infrastructure, while industry and academic consensus on the definition and criteria of AGI remains divided."